update public.fields set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='$\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{GW}}$ & l/d & Total greywater inflow | The daily total greywater inflow can be determined using Table 2 (see Section 5) und is calculated from the subtotals of the single greywater sources according to the following formula: | \mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{GW}}=\Sigma\left(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{GW}-\mathrm{P}, \mathrm{i}} \cdot \mathrm{P}_{\mathrm{i}}\right) \quad(\mathrm{I} / \mathrm{d}) \tag{2} — printed p.10 / p.26', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§2.2 Symbols; §9.3 Eq.(2), printed p.10 / p.26 derived) [VC] — duplicate of ws 07; derived — Eq.(2) output', verified_at=now() where id='c30a7b4c-16c1-47a0-868d-8293bda45aa9' and verification_status not in ('verified_against_standard','corrected');

-- ######################## M277E-18 · Behandlungs-Kapazitaet & Wasserbilanz ########################
-- Q_GWT
update public.fields set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='$\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{GWT}}$ & l/d & Greywater treatment capacity | In addition to the greywater and service water qualities, the decisive dimensioning factor for a greywater reuse system is the necessary daily treatment capacity. The daily service water demand and greywater inflow should be compared with each other. Decisive for the hydraulic dimensioning is the smaller value in each case. | The treatment capacity can be determined as follows: | Q_{\mathrm{GWT}}=Q_{\mathrm{GW}} \text {, when } Q_{\mathrm{SW}}>Q_{\mathrm{GW}}(\mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d})  \tag{3} | Q_{\mathrm{GWT}}=Q_{\mathrm{SW}} \text {, when } Q_{\mathrm{GW}}>Q_{\mathrm{SW}}(\mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d}) \tag{4} — printed p.10 / p.27–28', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§2.2 Symbols; §9.4 Eq.(3)/(4), printed p.10 / p.27–28 derived) [VC] — duplicate of ws 08; derived', verified_at=now() where id='ad2cdfae-7b9a-4ce8-92b6-c2fc92adb535' and verification_status not in ('verified_against_standard','corrected');
-- Q_WB
update public.fields set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='$\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{WB}}$ & l/d & Water balance | The smaller the difference between demand and inflow, the more economical is the overall system. | Q_{W B}=Q_{G W}-Q_{S W}=0(l / d) | $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{WB}}$ l/d Water balance | This water balance can be optimised by including or excluding separated greywater flows and /or users. As a rule, a positive water balance can be determined ( $Q_{W B}>0$ ), which means that the amount of available daily greywater inflow is higher than the demand for service water. — printed p.10 / p.27–28', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§2.2 Symbols; §9.4 water balance, printed p.10 / p.27–28 derived) [VC] — duplicate of ws 08; derived', verified_at=now() where id='b3ff3725-4ffe-4ac6-b156-1ff5936fdabd' and verification_status not in ('verified_against_standard','corrected');

