-- M277E-18.Q_WB_base (7eb2b0e7-fed6-4e93-ab4a-0c3ca163966b) — seasonal split of the water balance is EKOWAI's — the source defines ONE daily Q_WB (§9.4) and, in the §9.2 example, folds irrigation into the daily demand (60 l/m² · 150 m² / 180 d); no base-period balance is printed
-- M277E-18.Q_WB_irrig (b33bc79d-4864-40c4-8624-2dafb8fffdde) — as Q_WB_base — no irrigation-period balance in the source
-- M277E-18.V_GW_annual (be117623-a081-4ca0-9f8b-76cd427665f3) — annual volume (m³/a) not defined; §9.4 asks only that the daily capacity "should be complemented with data on the weekly/monthly capacity"
-- M277E-18.V_SW_annual (ebe1252b-fe48-4687-9f88-d1eb9609f1f1) — annual service-water demand not defined in the source (daily Q_SW only, Eq.(1))
-- M277E-18.V_treated_annual (ff1ec918-200a-4f65-a9fd-b914b1319a50) — annual treated volume not defined in the source
-- M277E-18.V_surplus_annual (d9d4aab7-6e43-4846-9142-4d2b2fb1ca10) — the source defines only the daily surplus (Q_WB > 0, "must be drained via the overflow", §9.4); the annual m³/a quantity is EKOWAI's
-- M277E-18.V_topup_annual (6770dd14-c8eb-4b85-9bc7-c877f4bde834) — the source mentions "higher drinking water backfeed" for Q_WB < 0 qualitatively (§9.4); no annual top-up quantity is printed
-- M277E-18.drinking_water_savings_rate (45d14c62-4b70-4e82-8148-4f0f3030c61c) — no definition or formula; "drinking water substitution" (§9.4) and "reduce the drinking water consumption" (§3) are qualitative only

-- ######################## SECTION B — equations (22, all needs_engineer_review → verified_against_standard) ########################
-- Eq. (1) Q_SW (ws 06)
update public.equations set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='The daily total service water demand $\left(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{SW}}\right)$ can be calculated from the subtotals of the single application areas according to the following formula: | Q_{S W}=\Sigma\left(Q_{S W-P, i} \cdot P_{i}\right)+\Sigma\left(Q_{S W-A, j} \cdot A_{j}\right) \tag{1} | $Q_{\text {SW }}$ & $l / d$ & Total service water demand | $Q_{\text {SW-P }}$ & $l / d$ & Person-specific service water demand | $Q_{\text {SW-A }}$ & $l /\left(d \cdot m^{2}\right)$ & Area-specific service water demand | P & & Person/Resident | A & $m^{2}$ & Irrigation area — printed p.25', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§9.2 Eq.(1), printed p.25 derived) [VC] — formula verbatim as the md prints it (LaTeX); encoded SUM(Q_SW_P_i*P_i)+SUM(Q_SW_A_j*A_j) = the printed Σ form', verified_at=now() where id='ca5fdae7-753b-42a7-a3fa-80034935d7fd' and verification_status <> 'verified_against_standard';
-- Ex. 9.2 (ws 06)
update public.equations set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='Example calculation Part 1: Service water demand | Building object: household | Residents: 25 P | Service water consumers: toilets, irrigation of kitchen garden ( $150 \mathrm{~m}^{2}$, season with 180 days) | Q_{S W}=25 \mathrm{P} \cdot 33 \mathrm{l} /(\mathrm{P} \cdot \mathrm{~d}) \text { toilet }+60 \mathrm{l} / \mathrm{m}^{2} \cdot 150 \mathrm{~m}^{2} / 180 \mathrm{~d} \text { kitchen garden } | Q_{S W}=875 \mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d} | Minimum service water quality: C2 due to the irrigation of the kitchen garden. — printed p.25', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§9.2 Example calculation Part 1, printed p.25 derived) [VC] — worked example: 25·33 + 60·150/180 = 825 + 50 = 875 l/d — arithmetic re-checked', verified_at=now() where id='89340a0c-143a-4a2c-bba4-872906416e66' and verification_status <> 'verified_against_standard';
-- Eq. (2) Q_GW (ws 07)
update public.equations set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='The daily total greywater inflow can be determined using Table 2 (see Section 5) und is calculated from the subtotals of the single greywater sources according to the following formula: | \mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{GW}}=\Sigma\left(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{GW}-\mathrm{P}, \mathrm{i}} \cdot \mathrm{P}_{\mathrm{i}}\right) \quad(\mathrm{I} / \mathrm{d}) \tag{2} | $Q_{\text {GW }}$ & $l / d$ & Total greywater inflow | $Q_{\text {GW-P }}$ & $l / d$ & Person-specific greywater inflow | $P$ & & Person/Resident — printed p.26', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§9.3 Eq.(2), printed p.26 derived) [VC] — the md prints the unit as "(I / d)" (OCR of l/d)', verified_at=now() where id='513e8d90-bf9f-4255-93c3-784b37a178d5' and verification_status <> 'verified_against_standard';
-- Ex. 9.3-A1 (ws 07)
update public.equations set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='Example calculation Part 1: Greywater inflow A1 | Greywater sources: showers, bathtubs | Q_{G W}=25 \mathrm{P} \cdot 40 \mathrm{l} /(\mathrm{P} \cdot \mathrm{~d}) \text { shower }+25 \mathrm{P} \cdot 15 \mathrm{l} /(\mathrm{P} \cdot \mathrm{~d}) \text { bathtub } | Q_{G W}=1,375 \mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d} — printed p.26', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§9.3 Example calculation Part 1, printed p.26 derived) [VC] — 25·40 + 25·15 = 1,375 l/d — arithmetic re-checked; the example''s 40/15 l/(P·d) are in-range picks from Table 2', verified_at=now() where id='8e434536-7820-4038-9691-bb37b6a9a653' and verification_status <> 'verified_against_standard';
-- Ex. 9.3-A2 (ws 07)
update public.equations set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='Example calculation Part 2: Greywater inflow A2 | Greywater sources: shower, bathtub, hand washbasin | Q_{G W}= & 25 \mathrm{P} \cdot 40 \mathrm{l} /(\mathrm{P} \cdot \mathrm{~d}) \text { shower }+25 \mathrm{P} \cdot 15 \mathrm{l} /(\mathrm{P} \cdot \mathrm{~d}) \text { bathtub } | & +25 \mathrm{P} \cdot 10 \mathrm{l} /(\mathrm{P} \cdot \mathrm{~d}) \text { hand washbasin } | Q_{G W}= & 1,625 \mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d} — printed p.26', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§9.3 Example calculation Part 2, printed p.26 derived) [VC] — + 25·10 = 1,625 l/d — arithmetic re-checked', verified_at=now() where id='dd0ccdaf-8fa5-4b04-973f-45604e1184df' and verification_status <> 'verified_against_standard';
-- Eq. (3) Q_GWT (ws 08)
update public.equations set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='In addition to the greywater and service water qualities, the decisive dimensioning factor for a greywater reuse system is the necessary daily treatment capacity. The daily service water demand and greywater inflow should be compared with each other. Decisive for the hydraulic dimensioning is the smaller value in each case. | The treatment capacity can be determined as follows: | Q_{\mathrm{GWT}}=Q_{\mathrm{GW}} \text {, when } Q_{\mathrm{SW}}>Q_{\mathrm{GW}}(\mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d})  \tag{3} | Q_{\mathrm{GWT}}=Q_{\mathrm{SW}} \text {, when } Q_{\mathrm{GW}}>Q_{\mathrm{SW}}(\mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d}) \tag{4} — printed p.27–28', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§9.4 Eq.(3), printed p.27–28 derived) [VC] — the source prints (3) and (4) as two conditional assignments; the encoded min(Q_GW, Q_SW) is their equivalent — the Q_GW = Q_SW case is not printed (both branches then coincide)', verified_at=now() where id='b1d09312-e8b8-4087-bed6-35170514b298' and verification_status <> 'verified_against_standard';
-- Eq. (4) Q_GWT (ws 08)
update public.equations set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='In addition to the greywater and service water qualities, the decisive dimensioning factor for a greywater reuse system is the necessary daily treatment capacity. The daily service water demand and greywater inflow should be compared with each other. Decisive for the hydraulic dimensioning is the smaller value in each case. | The treatment capacity can be determined as follows: | Q_{\mathrm{GWT}}=Q_{\mathrm{GW}} \text {, when } Q_{\mathrm{SW}}>Q_{\mathrm{GW}}(\mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d})  \tag{3} | Q_{\mathrm{GWT}}=Q_{\mathrm{SW}} \text {, when } Q_{\mathrm{GW}}>Q_{\mathrm{SW}}(\mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d}) \tag{4} — printed p.27–28', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§9.4 Eq.(4), printed p.27–28 derived) [VC] — same note as Eq.(3); both prod rows carry the same min() formula', verified_at=now() where id='5389af5f-a563-4401-9035-74223ec27f03' and verification_status <> 'verified_against_standard';
-- Ex. 9.4 (ws 08)
update public.equations set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='Example calculation Part 3: Treatment capacity | Q_{\mathrm{SW}}=875 \mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d} | Q_{\mathrm{GW}}=1,625 \mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d} | \text { since } Q_{\mathrm{GW}}>Q_{\mathrm{SW}} \text { then } Q_{\mathrm{GWT}}=Q_{\mathrm{SW}}=875 \mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d} — printed p.27–28', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§9.4 Example calculation Part 3, printed p.27–28 derived) [VC] — since Q_GW (1,625) > Q_SW (875) → Q_GWT = Q_SW = 875 l/d', verified_at=now() where id='7f92ffee-c3a3-461a-9dae-175a947c6676' and verification_status <> 'verified_against_standard';
-- Ex. 9.4-WB (ws 08)
update public.equations set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='Determination and evaluation of the water balance: | Q_{W B}=1,625 \mathrm{~L} / \mathrm{d}-875 \mathrm{~L} / \mathrm{d} | Q_{W B}=+750 \mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d} | Greywater surplus $\left(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{WB}}=+750 \mathrm{l} / \mathrm{d}\right)$ : - Other service water consumers can be connected to the service water network. - It is not compulsory to connect all greywater sources to the greywater treatment system. - The greywater surplus must be drained via the overflow of the greywater treatment/reuse system. — printed p.27–28', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§9.4 Example calculation Part 3, printed p.27–28 derived) [VC] — 1,625 − 875 = +750 l/d — arithmetic re-checked; md prints "~L/d" here', verified_at=now() where id='8ed7e9e9-b247-482a-924c-d1e7f5a435fd' and verification_status <> 'verified_against_standard';
-- QWB (ws 08)
update public.equations set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='The smaller the difference between demand and inflow, the more economical is the overall system. | Q_{W B}=Q_{G W}-Q_{S W}=0(l / d) | $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{WB}}$ l/d Water balance | This water balance can be optimised by including or excluding separated greywater flows and /or users. As a rule, a positive water balance can be determined ( $Q_{W B}>0$ ), which means that the amount of available daily greywater inflow is higher than the demand for service water. — printed p.27–28', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§9.4 water balance, printed p.27–28 derived) [VC] — printed without an equation number; "= 0" is the optimum, not part of the definition', verified_at=now() where id='554b0a01-3356-4a62-9780-fd727a569add' and verification_status <> 'verified_against_standard';
-- Buffer V_buffer (ws 09)
update public.equations set verification_status='verified_against_standard', verification_quote='A trouble-free operation is only secured in a frost-free installation. Depending on the type of technology used, storage of the greywater should be provided for before and/or after treatment. It is recommended that the total buffer volume corresponds to one-day treatment capacity. — printed p.22', verification_note='md-verified 2026-09-05 (§7.1, printed p.22 derived) [VC] — the source prints NO formula — "It is recommended that the total buffer volume corresponds to one-day treatment capacity"; V_buffer = Q_GWT · 1 d is EKOWAI''s rendering of that recommendation (l = l/d · 1 d)', verified_at=now() where id='4df4256f-4098-4b6f-94bd-e5c67276d3a8' and verification_status <> 'verified_against_standard';
